{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2156 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "496610db0557131d81bb35cc9232d979ad82c3862d21de9654cbb1df3b876322" :edb/db-before-t 2155 :edb/db-after-t 2156 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535468 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:41:13.920Z" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124216 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: | \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124216 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: \\mid \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under function addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" 13194139535468 false] [17592186124216 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A$, then\n$(f + g)(0)|= f(0) + g(0); =-f(2) - g(2); = - (f(2) + g(2)); = - (f + g)(2)$,\nwhich means $f+g \\in A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$(kf)(0)|= kf(0); = k \\cdot (-f(2)); =-kf(2); = - (kf)(2)$,\nwhich means $kf \\in A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535468 false] [17592186124216 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =-f(2)-g(2) \\\\  & =-(f(2)+g(2)) \\\\  & =-(f+g)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $f+g \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =kf(0) \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-f(2)) \\\\  & =-kf(2) \\\\  & =-(kf)(2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means $kf \\in A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124319 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$, and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n2. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n4. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" 13194139535468 false] [17592186124319 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R},$ and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124319 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0$. Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$. This matrix belongs to $N$, and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0$. If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0$. This matrix does not belong to $N$, and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139535468 false] [17592186124319 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0.$ Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$ This matrix belongs to $N,$  and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0.$ If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0.$ This matrix does not belong to $N,$  and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124320 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3$, and its subset $A = \\big\\{at^3-bt \\: \\mid \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" 13194139535468 false] [17592186124320 1100 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ at^3-bt \\: | \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124320 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A$, then\n$f(t) + g(t)|= (a_{1}t^{3} - b_{1}t) + (a_{2}t^{3} - b_{2}t); = (a_{1} + a_{2})t^{3} - (b_{1} + b_{2})t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$kf(t)|= k(at^{3} - bt); = (ka)t^{3} - (kb)t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535468 false] [17592186124320 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}-{b}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}-{b}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{3}-bt) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{3}-(kb)t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535468 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350679 13194139535468 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350686 13194139535468 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350700 13194139535468 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350742 13194139535468 true] [17592186124406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350749 13194139535468 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350665 13194139535468 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350672 13194139535468 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350721 13194139535468 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350728 13194139535468 true] [17592186124511 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350735 13194139535468 true] [17592186124512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350658 13194139535468 true] [17592186124512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350693 13194139535468 true] [17592186124512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350707 13194139535468 true] [17592186124512 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350714 13194139535468 true] [17592186125069 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535468 true] [17592186125069 1100 "Consider the vector space $R_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$, and its subset $B = {t^{2} + at + b \\mid a, b ∈ R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $B$ is a subspace of $R_{2}[t]$\n4. $B$ is a **not** a subspace of $R_{2}[t]$" 13194139535468 false] [17592186125069 1100 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B={{t}^{2}+at+b\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $B$ is a **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" 13194139535468 true] [17592186125069 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)={t}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)={t}^{2}$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350658 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350659 13194139535468 true] [17592186350658 1089 "q-107102" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350658 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535468 true] [17592186350658 1100 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350658 1113 #uuid "c442e1c1-38ed-511a-a1b1-3b78d327f498" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350658 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}+b$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{5}+b) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5}+(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350660 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350661 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350662 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350663 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350664 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1041 "selection" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350662 13194139535468 true] [17592186350659 1110 #uuid "e596d731-aa76-5197-b9aa-9f951e538408" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350660 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350660 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535468 true] [17592186350660 1111 #uuid "23748f69-587f-53ef-8dc8-a9f7f3578dd5" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350661 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535468 true] [17592186350661 1111 #uuid "116aba5d-3108-5126-930e-9b95f3df2a62" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350662 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535468 true] [17592186350662 1111 #uuid "0d7c3e6d-8d14-5705-8193-ea40dc5f72da" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350663 1034 "IV only" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535468 true] [17592186350663 1111 #uuid "afc7beb1-5e7f-5b84-99b3-5e64779e7a24" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350664 1034 "II only" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535468 true] [17592186350664 1111 #uuid "5045511f-8b47-5700-b7c4-a4a7515c2050" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350666 13194139535468 true] [17592186350665 1089 "q-107115" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535468 true] [17592186350665 1100 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350665 1113 #uuid "b6393c24-bcf5-559b-abd5-9923066a7bff" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350665 1145 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350667 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350668 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350669 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350670 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350671 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1041 "selection" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350670 13194139535468 true] [17592186350666 1110 #uuid "a6b30a02-5870-5daa-b785-941270bb098a" 13194139535468 true] [17592186350667 1034 "I and IV only" 131